.TH CBMAP_CONSTRUCT 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
cbmap_construct \- type-inferred BST map construction
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cbstmap.h>

cbmap_construct(hm_name, key_type, val_type);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a BST map with the default settings. You don't name the kind of
key: the macro works out whether key_type is a signed integer,
an unsigned integer, a floating-point type, a string or some other type.
It also creates the type marker variables, which carry the declared key
type and value type to the other type-dispatching macros wherever you
call them.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
hm_name   Name of the BST map variable to create
key_type  Type of the keys
val_type  Type of the values
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The macro stops the program when an allocation fails.

The map gets the default memory management and the default comparison.
A struct key_type is ordered by a raw memcmp of its bytes; see
cbmap_create_full(3) for the rules of the default comparison and for how
to pass a custom_comparison_proc instead. For a struct key_type the
type-inferred macro to use is cbmap_construct_cc.

An array key type or value type, such as int[2] or char[16], does not
compile, and the error message tells you to wrap the array in a struct.
The macros copy keys and values by assignment, which C doesn't allow for
an array, so the map would end up storing the address of the caller's
array instead of its elements. A struct that holds the array works.
.fi
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
cbmap_construct(map, int, char*);
int key = 42;
cbmap_insert(map, key, "hello");
cbmap_destroy(map);
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR cbmap_create (3),
.BR cbmap_create_full (3)
